<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peace of Zsitvatorok</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1606">1606</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Treaty of Zsitvatorok · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-turkish-war">Long Turkish War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Peace of Zsitvatorok was signed on 11 November 1606 at the mouth of the Zsitva River in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-turkish-war">Long Turkish War</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg Monarchy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. It confirmed the existing frontier, replaced the Habsburgs' annual tribute with a single payment, and for the first time had the sultan address the Habsburg ruler as emperor, an equal. The peace held for over half a century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peace-of-zsitvatorok.jpg" alt="Peace of Zsitvatorok" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Szeder László · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zsitvat%C5%91.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Peace of Zsitvatorok, also called the Treaty of Sitvatorok, ended the thirteen-year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-turkish-war">Long Turkish War</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy. It belonged to a system of peace treaties that also ended the anti-Habsburg uprising of Stephen Bocskai, which lasted from 1604 to 1606. It was negotiated between 24 October and 11 November 1606 at the former mouth of the Žitava River, called Zsitva in Hungarian, where it flows into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-hungary">Royal Hungary</a>, now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>. The place later became the small settlement of Žitavská Tôňa. Sultan Ahmed I and Archduke Matthias of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> signed on behalf of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, and Matthias's brother, the Emperor Rudolf II, ratified the treaty on 9 December.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The peace was signed for twenty years. Diplomatic historians have interpreted it in different ways, partly because the Ottoman Turkish and Hungarian texts differ. The Hungarian text had a payment of 200,000 florins as a once-and-for-all tribute in place of the annual tribute of 30,000 guldens paid before the war, whereas the Ottoman text foresaw that the payment would be repeated after three years. The treaty prohibited Ottoman looting campaigns into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-hungary">Royal Hungary</a> and allowed Hungarian settlements under Ottoman rule to collect taxes themselves through village judges. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> also acknowledged the tax-free privilege of nobles, though they never really complied with these terms. The Crimean Khanate was explicitly included as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Ottoman Empire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>' inability to penetrate further into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-hungary">Royal Hungary</a> during the war was one of their first geopolitical defeats. Even so, the treaty stabilised the Habsburg-Ottoman frontier for half a century to the benefit of both sides: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> faced serious domestic opposition in the following years, while the Ottomans, apart from internal rebellion, had open conflicts on other frontiers, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</p><p>The treaty also mattered for protocol. The sultan, who had borne the title Kayser-i Rûm, Caesar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, since the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, titled the Holy Roman Emperor Padishah, one of his own titles, for the first time. Until then Ottoman diplomacy had regarded the emperor as merely the kral, king, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> read this as an acceptance of a divisio imperii between West and East. The next European ruler granted such respect was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kucuk-kaynarca">Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca</a> of 1774.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peace of Zsitvatorok” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The inconclusive Long Turkish War of 1593–1606 ended with the <b>Peace of Zsitvatorok</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Military</a></li></ul></section>
